## 1. Git workflow (mandatory, no exceptions)

- Base branch: `main` (only long-lived branch). Always cut new branches from an
  up-to-date `main` (`git pull --ff-only` before branching).
- Branch naming: `v1.x.y`
  - Increment `x` for feature, improvement or new logic. Reset `y` to `0`.
  - Increment `y` for fix, hotfix or bug.
- Current baseline: `v1.0.0` (tagged on `main` HEAD).
- Commits: Conventional Commits in English with a short scope. Allowed types:
  `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `perf`, `build`, `ci`.
  Example: `feat(dashboard): replace heuristic KPI formulas`.
- One feature branch per logical change. Keep diffs minimal and focused.
- PR target: `main`. Open via `gh pr create` whenever the CLI is installed and
  authenticated. Otherwise push the branch and surface the compare URL.
- Never use destructive git commands (force push, hard reset against shared
  history, branch deletion on remote) without explicit user request.

### Branch-only coordination docs (`docs/coordination/`)

- Per-release Markdown pair: `vX.Y.Z-frontend-sync.md` and `vX.Y.Z-backend-sync.md`
  (plus templates). Used so frontend and backend workstreams understand each
  other on the **same** `v1.x.y` branch.
- **Never merge** `docs/coordination/**` into `main`. Before merging a PR to
  `main`, remove that directory from the diff (e.g. `git rm -r docs/coordination`)
  or exclude it from the squash/cherry-pick. Pushing these files to `origin/v*`
  is expected for remote visibility during the release line.
- Canonical policy: [docs/coordination/README.md](docs/coordination/README.md).

### Deliverable format for every PR (copy-paste ready)

Always return to the user the following four blocks, in this order:

1. PR URL — created via `gh pr create` when possible, otherwise the GitHub
   compare URL
   `https://github.com/gregoryfiel/gestao_lancamento_de_chamados/compare/main...<branch>?expand=1`.
2. Title (English): `vX.Y.Z — <conventional commit subject>`
   Example: `v1.1.0 — docs: add frontend agent rule with project context`.
3. Body (English) with these sections:
   - `## Summary` — what changed and why.
   - `## Changes` — bullet list of concrete edits and new files.
   - `## Validation` — commands run (lint, build, manual checks) and outcomes.
   - `## Out of scope` — explicit non-goals to prevent review drift.
4. Approval comment in pt-BR, short and direct, ready to paste in the PR.

